Bound the DSP's output buffer.

`Dsp::_dspOutput` is a fixed `int16_t[0x2000]` that `Dsp::Exec` appends two
samples to per output cycle, advancing `_outSampleCount` with no bound check.
The only thing that empties it is `Spc::ProcessEndFrame`, which runs at the end
of a frame.

Every debugger break tops the audio up on the way in --
`SnesDebugger::OnBeforeBreak` calls `SnesConsole::RunAudio()` -- so a debugger
that breaks repeatedly without letting a frame finish keeps filling a buffer
nothing drains. After about eight frames' worth, `Dsp::Exec` writes past the end
of its own heap object. AddressSanitizer reports a two-byte
heap-buffer-overflow at `Dsp.cpp:320`; glibc reports it later and elsewhere as a
corrupt heap, so the fault lands on whatever allocation comes next rather than
on the guilty one.

Neither `_outSampleCount` nor `_dspOutput` is serialized, so restoring a
savestate does not reset the count. That is what makes it reachable from a host
which drives cartridge routines out of band and rewinds the clock between them,
as this editor's sprite capture does: the console is walked back from the frame
boundary it would have drained at, repeatedly, while the buffer keeps filling.

Dropping samples once the buffer is full is the conservative fix. It can only be
reached when a frame has not ended for eight frames' worth of emulated audio,
which no unattended emulation does.

--- a/Core/SNES/DSP/Dsp.cpp
+++ b/Core/SNES/DSP/Dsp.cpp
@@ -311,19 +311,25 @@
 			//"Output the right sample to the DAC."
 			EchoStep27();
 
-			if(ReadReg(DspGlobalRegs::Flags) & 0x40) {
-				//Global mute/silence flag
-				_dspOutput[_outSampleCount] = 0;
-				_dspOutput[_outSampleCount + 1] = 0;
-			} else {
-				_dspOutput[_outSampleCount] = (int16_t)_state.OutSamples[0];
-				_dspOutput[_outSampleCount + 1] = (int16_t)_state.OutSamples[1];
+			//Only ProcessEndFrame empties this buffer, and a debugger that
+			//breaks without letting a frame finish keeps filling it, because
+			//OnBeforeBreak catches audio up on every break. Drop samples
+			//rather than write past the end.
+			if(_outSampleCount + 1 < DspOutputSize) {
+				if(ReadReg(DspGlobalRegs::Flags) & 0x40) {
+					//Global mute/silence flag
+					_dspOutput[_outSampleCount] = 0;
+					_dspOutput[_outSampleCount + 1] = 0;
+				} else {
+					_dspOutput[_outSampleCount] = (int16_t)_state.OutSamples[0];
+					_dspOutput[_outSampleCount + 1] = (int16_t)_state.OutSamples[1];
+				}
+
+				_outSampleCount += 2;
 			}
 
 			_state.OutSamples[0] = 0;
 			_state.OutSamples[1] = 0;
-
-			_outSampleCount += 2;
 			break;
 
 		case 28:
